1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Let's get into the constraints that shape a cloud application before features.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Can this design hit its latency, throughput, region, and cost targets? Those are infrastructure

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
requirements and they decide the architecture. This lesson covers how to turn them into numbers

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
you can design against. Let's dive in. Start by separating two kinds of requirement

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
because mixing them is a classic mistake. A functional requirement states what the application

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
has to do. Summarize this document. Answer in this format. Those come from the business need

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
and they describe the behavior a user gets. An infrastructure requirement is different.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
It states what the solution architecture has to provide to support that behavior. Compute,

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
latency, throughput, hosting. The trap is listing a behavior when an infrastructure constraint is

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
really what drives the design. Respond in under two seconds isn't a feature. It's a latency budget

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
and it will shape your whole architecture more than the wording of the prompt ever will.

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Infrastructure requirements group into four families, so name them early. The first is latency.

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
How long will a caller wait before the design has failed them? The second is throughput. How many

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
requests per minute does the integration edge have to sustain at peak? The third is region and

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
residency. Where do the calls physically run and where is the data allowed to live? The fourth is

16
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
cost. What ceiling does the bill have to stay inside? Your model tier choice threads through

17
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:42,000
two of these since a heavier tier buys quality at the price of latency and cost. And availability,

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
the uptime commitment, returns in the next lesson as its own reliability requirement. Each of these

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
is a number you can write down. When they're vague, the architecture is guessing. When they're

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
concrete, the design almost falls out of them. A latency budget is a specific number, not a wish.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Before you pick a design, decide the worst case response time a user will actually tolerate.

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Then subtract your own overhead, network hops, authentication, any processing you do before and

23
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
after the model call. Whatever is left is the budget the cloud call itself has to fit inside.

24
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
This matters because the budget quietly rules designs in or out. If you've got a second and a

25
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
half of headroom, a multi-step agent loop that makes five sequential calls is already off the

26
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:39,000
table. A large context that takes longer to process might be too. So work backward from the

27
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
number. Let the latency budget tell you how many calls and how much context you can afford instead

28
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
of discovering it in production. Once you have a budget, you choose how the response is delivered.

29
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
And there are three shapes. Synchronous is the simplest. One request, one response. And the

30
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
caller waits for the whole thing. Easy to build, but the user feels every millisecond. Streaming

31
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
sends tokens incrementally over one connection. So output starts appearing almost right away,

32
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
even if the complete answer still takes a while. That's often how you meet a perceived latency

33
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:20,000
target without making the model any faster. Batch is the other direction entirely. You submit many

34
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
requests together and collect them later, trading real-time speed for lower cost on work nobody is

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
sitting and waiting for. The budget picks the mode. A chat reply wants streaming. An overnight

36
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:39,000
report wants batch. Throughput is where your design collides with real limits. The API enforces rate

37
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
limits, caps on how many requests, and how many tokens you get in a given window. When you exceed

38
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
one, you get a rate limit response, which is a signal to slow down and try again after a wait.

39
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,000
The next lesson sorts out exactly which errors deserve that retry treatment.

40
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:03,000
The deeper pattern is back pressure. When incoming demand outruns the capacity you have,

41
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,000
you cue the excess work instead of firing it all at the model and getting throttled. Size

42
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
your concurrency to the rate limit so the integration edge stays smooth. And always

43
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
plan for the busy minute at peak because the average minute never breaks anything.

44
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Region and data residency can decide where your calls physically run. Sometimes a contract or a

45
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
regulation says user data has to stay inside a particular geography. That constraint shapes the

46
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
architecture directly. One path is the first party API. You call the provider's own endpoint,

47
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
which is the simplest integration, but you inherit whichever regions that endpoint serves.

48
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:50,000
The other path is running Claude inside a major cloud platform you already operate in. That option

49
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
can pin traffic to a specific region, which is often exactly what a data residency rule requires.

50
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Neither is better in the abstract. If residency is a hard requirement, it can outweigh convenience

51
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
and push you toward the deployment that keeps the data where the rules say it has to live.

52
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Sometimes the budget that binds hardest isn't the clock, it's the bill. Token cost scales with your

53
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
context size and your turn count. A loop that resends a growing history gets expensive fast,

54
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
so a tight cost envelope can forbid a design outright, the same way a tight latency budget can.

55
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Here's the mindset shift. When volume is high enough, caching a stable prompt prefix stops being

56
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,000
a nice optimization and becomes a requirement, because without it, the numbers simply don't work.

57
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Same with deferrable bulk work. If nobody is waiting on it, running it in a cheaper batch

58
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
lane isn't polish. It's how you stay inside the ceiling. Treat the cost limit as a first-class

59
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:57,000
requirement right next to latency and throughput. So how do you actually hit a tight budget? Three

60
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:02,000
levers do most of the work. The first is prompt caching. When a big chunk of your prompt stays

61
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:09,000
the same across calls, you cache that prefix and repeated reads bill at a fraction of the base

62
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:15,000
input price. Cache writes bill at a premium, so this only pays off when the prefix is actually

63
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:23,000
reused. Reused often, it cuts cost and time to first token, so it helps latency too. The second

64
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
is message batches. Deferrable work runs asynchronously at half the standard price

65
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,000
collected within a day. The catch is results come back in any order, so you match them by

66
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,000
identifier, not position. The third is right sizing. Match the model tier to the task,

67
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
so you never pay for reasoning a light job doesn't need. Pull the levers, the budget demands.

68
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Here's the bottom line. Infrastructure requirements are constraints your architecture

69
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:01,000
has to satisfy, not just features. Name a real latency budget, then let it choose sync,

70
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:07,000
streaming, or batch. Plan throughput around rate limits and back pressure. Just remember,

71
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,000
when the cost ceiling is tight, caching and batching are requirements, not optional polish.

72
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:18,000
And that covers latency, throughput, and regional budgets. I'll see you in the next lesson.

